{"conditionalSetIds":[],"forecasterId":"demographics-ai-2036:run1:growth:audit","informationSetArtifactId":"sha256:cec06cf76b5a8585fe2d7693d81574ea9b7c67bfd26e59266b4067989647beaf","modelForecastIds":[],"modelUse":"none","prediction":{"kind":"categorical","probabilities":{"A":0.11,"B":0.5,"C":0.28,"D":0.11}},"questionHash":"sha256:ca2a4311940635fba34c40e7278721deb2f4716732e6011c94e6eb0554263e61","questionId":"demographics-ai-2036-cq6","questionVersion":"1.0.0","rationale":"LOCK CONFIRMED AND AUDITED. I apply the stated lexical priority exactly: (1) A if coupled growth >= 2.6; (2) else C if OECD-38 GDP-weighted core >= 2.7 AND growth < 2.0; (3) else D if growth < 1.7 AND core < 2.0; (4) else B. The rule is well-formed and exhaustive: step 1 partitions on growth alone, steps 2 and 3 are disjoint on core (>= 2.7 vs < 2.0), and step 4 absorbs everything else. Confirmed MECE under the lock.\nCRITICAL: the CQ6 inflation measure is the SAME \"GDP-weighted OECD-38 core\" object audited in CQ2, so the Turkey/Mexico/Poland PPP-GDP-weight artefact (Turkey ~3.5-4% weight at even 15% core contributes ~+0.55 pp to the aggregate) applies here too. This is the single biggest driver of my CQ6 shape and it pushes mass from D into C and B.\nSTEP 1 - A. From my CQ3 (P10 1.10 / P50 1.85 / P90 2.65), P(growth >= 2.6) ~ 0.11. A = 11. Consistency check stated explicitly: my CQ3 P50 is 1.85%, so I cannot and do not put most mass on A; A sits just under my P90 exceedance.\nSTEP 2 - C. P(core >= 2.7 over 2032-36) = 0.70 x 0.60 (literal all-38 construction) + 0.30 x 0.22 (ex-high-inflation construction) = 0.486. P(growth < 2.0) = 0.60 from my CQ3 distribution. Mild positive dependence in a bottleneck world, factor 1.05: P(C) = 0.486 x 0.60 x 1.05 = 0.306, trimmed to 0.28 for the chance that a resolver who already chose an ex-Turkey construction in CQ2 applies it consistently here.\nSTEP 3 - D. P(growth < 1.7) = 0.42. P(core < 2.0) = 0.70 x 0.10 + 0.30 x 0.42 = 0.196 - the all-38 aggregate almost cannot print below 2.0% with Turkey and Mexico in it, which is why D is crushed relative to the intuitive \"capex bust\" story. Positive correlation factor 1.3: P(D) = 0.42 x 0.196 x 1.3 = 0.107 -> 11.\nSTEP 4 - B RESIDUAL = 100 - 11 - 28 - 11 = 50.\nCONSISTENCY CHECK AGAINST CQ2. My CQ2 is 50, driven by a 0.75 marginal on OECD core >= 2.50% over 2027-2036. A 0.486 probability on the stricter >= 2.70% over the narrower 2032-2036 window is coherent with that (higher bar, shorter window, same construction artefact). If I had scored CQ2's inflation conjunct at a Germany-weighted ~0.45, C would fall to ~0.17 and B would rise to ~0.61 - I state this as the explicit sensitivity.\nRESIDUAL-B ABSORPTION QUANTIFIED (lens requirement). B's literal band is growth 1.7-2.5%/yr, which carries only ~0.28-0.30 of my growth mass. B's assigned 0.50 therefore absorbs ~20-22 pp that a naive reader would have placed elsewhere, in two identifiable cells: (i) growth < 1.7% with core between 2.0% and 2.7% - approximately 0.42 x 0.32 = ~13-14 pp - satisfies NO literal definition and a naive reader files it under D; (ii) growth between 2.0% and 2.6% with core >= 2.7% - approximately ~8 pp - is a stagflationary outcome that the lock sends to B because step 2 requires growth < 2.0, and a naive reader files it under C. Net: naive D is overstated by ~13-14 pp and naive C by ~8 pp relative to the lock. A reader who scored this without the lock would plausibly report something like A 12 / B 30 / C 33 / D 25; the lock mechanically moves ~20 pp into B.","referenceClassIds":[],"schemaVersion":"forecast-workbench.forecast/v1","sealedAt":"2026-11-04T17:31:54.546Z","seriesId":"published-panel","strongestContraryCase":"Not elicited per question. Panelist falsification signposts: {\"signpost\":\"Eurostat publishes the Structure of Earnings Survey 2026 wave (median gross hourly earnings by NACE section, reference year 2026) and the F+Q vs J+K+M median gap moves by less than 2 pp versus SES 2022 - or Eurostat/the OECD confirms no sectoral real median hourly wage aggregate will be constructed for OECD-38.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2030 (SES 2026 microdata/standard tables release)\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"CQ1 falls from 12 to 5-7: it confirms both that the composition drag neutralizes the F+Q advantage and that the 2036 endpoint is unobservable. If instead the gap has already opened by >= 4 pp in four years, CQ1 rises to 20-24.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"The OECD's published CPI-excluding-food-and-energy aggregate for the OECD total: whether the OECD continues to publish the all-38 aggregate including Turkey, or introduces/promotes an 'OECD excluding high-inflation members' or 'advanced-economy' core aggregate as the headline object.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2029\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"If an ex-high-inflation core aggregate becomes the standard published series, CQ2 falls from 50 to ~32 and CQ6 shifts C 28 -> 17, D 11 -> 18, B 50 -> 54. If the all-38 aggregate remains the only published core, CQ2 rises to ~56 and C holds at 28-31. This is the largest single resolution-mechanics lever in my whole payload.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"US BLS OES annual median hourly wage for NAICS 62 (health care and social assistance) relative to the all-industry median, plus the share of NAICS 62 employment in the three lowest-paid occupational groups (home health/personal care aides, nursing assistants).\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2031 (OES reference year 2030)\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"If the aide share of NAICS 62 employment has risen >= 3 pp while the NAICS 62 median lags the all-industry median, my CQ1 composition artefact is confirmed and CQ1 drops to 8. If the aide share is flat and the NAICS 62 median outpaces by >= 1 pp/yr, CQ1 rises to 18-20.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"Cumulative OECD-38 + China strict operational data-centre electricity consumption reported by IEA/national regulators, together with FERC/ENTSO-E/CEC interconnection queue clearance times and HV transformer lead times.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2031\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"If DC consumption is tracking above ~700 TWh by 2031 while interconnection queue times and transformer lead times are FALLING, the weakest-link cap is not binding: CQ3 P50 rises 1.85 -> 2.10, P90 2.65 -> 3.00, CQ6-A 11 -> 17. If DC consumption is tracking above 700 TWh while queue times lengthen and electrician wage growth accelerates, CQ3 P50 is unchanged but CQ6-C rises 28 -> 34 and CQ1's second conjunct gains ~4 pp.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"The first MSCI World peak-to-trough drawdown of >= 20% after Jan 1 2027, and the sign and magnitude of the change in G7 GDP-weighted 10y nominal yields between the equity peak and trough dates; plus the trailing 24-month average MOVE at that point.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2032\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"If yields RISE or fall by less than 50 bp through that drawdown, CQ5 rises from 26 to ~45-50 (conjunct 2 largely satisfied, and the episode lifts the MOVE average). If yields fall >= 100 bp in a clean flight-to-quality and trailing MOVE is below 90, CQ5 falls to ~14 and CQ4's P10 drops toward 0.6.\"}","updateBasis":{"kind":"launch","note":"run1 wave 1 audit seat, effort 'growth'."}}